## Configuration

Set your key in `.env` before the first run:

```bash
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
LLM_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5
LLM_MAX_TOKENS=4096
MAX_TOOL_ITERATIONS=5
```

The key is read by pydantic-settings and handed to the client at
construction — it is never written into source or into the Docker image.

To run against an Anthropic-compatible gateway (LiteLLM, an internal proxy,
a local server) instead of the Anthropic API, set `LLM_BASE_URL` and, if the
gateway uses bearer auth, `LLM_AUTH_TOKEN`. The wire format stays the
Anthropic Messages API; a gateway is what translates for other providers.

## Running the app

```bash
# create a virtualenv and install dependencies
$ uv venv && uv pip install -r requirements.txt

# launch the dev server
$ bash scripts/run-server.sh

# talk to it
$ bash scripts/chat.sh "What is 12 * 34?"
```

API docs are then served at:

- Swagger UI: <http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs>
- ReDoc:      <http://127.0.0.1:8000/redoc>

## API endpoints

| Method | Endpoint                                | Description                       |
|--------|-----------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|
| GET    | `/api/v1/health`                        | Liveness probe                    |
| POST   | `/api/v1/chat`                          | Stream a reply as SSE             |
| GET    | `/api/v1/conversations/{id}`            | Read stored history               |
| DELETE | `/api/v1/conversations/{id}`            | Forget a conversation             |

`POST /api/v1/chat` takes `{"message": "...", "conversation_id": "..."}` —
omit `conversation_id` to start a new conversation and read it back from the
final `done` event.

### Stream events

Each SSE message carries the event type in its `event:` field and a JSON
envelope in `data:`:

| Event         | Fields                              | Meaning                        |
|---------------|-------------------------------------|--------------------------------|
| `token`       | `text`                              | A chunk of the assistant reply |
| `tool_use`    | `tool_name`, `tool_input`           | Claude requested a tool        |
| `tool_result` | `tool_name`, `tool_output`          | The tool ran                   |
| `usage`       | `input_tokens`, `output_tokens`     | Per-turn token spend           |
| `done`        | `conversation_id`                   | The turn finished              |
| `error`       | `detail`                            | Something failed mid-stream    |

Token usage is also written to the `app.agent` logger on every turn, so
spend is visible in the server logs without a client.

## Bundled tools

| Tool           | Purpose                                                    |
|----------------|------------------------------------------------------------|
| `calculator`   | Arithmetic, parsed to an AST and whitelisted — not `eval`  |
| `current_time` | Current time in an IANA timezone                            |

To add one, append a schema to `TOOL_SCHEMAS` and register the handler in
`TOOL_HANDLERS` (`src/app/llm/tools.py`). The description is the only
instruction Claude gets about when to reach for it, so write it carefully.
Handlers should return a string and should turn their own failures into a
readable message rather than raising — the loop feeds that text straight back
to the model.

## Running tests

```bash
$ bash scripts/test.sh
# or directly:
$ pytest
```

`tests/conftest.py` replays scripted turns through a fake that mimics
`AsyncAnthropic.messages.stream`, so the suite is deterministic, offline, and
free.

## Extension points

- **Persistent history** — implement `ConversationStore` against Redis or a
  database and return it from `get_store()` in `src/app/api/deps.py`.
- **RAG** — retrieval is deliberately out of scope here. The natural seam is
  `ChatAgent.stream`: fetch context before the first request and prepend it to
  the system prompt, or expose your index as one more tool so the model
  decides when to search.
- **Auth and rate limiting** — `POST /chat` is unauthenticated as shipped.
  Put a dependency on the router before exposing it publicly.

